<HelpTab>
    <FluentText>
        <a href="/rota">My Shifts</a> is where you see when you're working, ask for changes, book time
        off and tell your managers when you can't work. Your manager builds the rota. You only see a
        shift once they've published it.
    </FluentText>

    <HelpSection Title="The tabs">
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Upcoming shifts</strong>: your published shifts for the next six weeks, grouped by week with the
                total hours. Each shift shows the time, position, location, any unpaid break and notes.
                Today's shift is marked <strong>Today</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Up for grabs</strong>: open shifts you could pick up, swaps you could help with, and how your
                own requests are going.</li>
            <li><strong>My hours</strong>: the time you've clocked over the last three weeks.</li>
            <li><strong>Time off</strong>: your allowance and your time-off requests.</li>
            <li><strong>Availability</strong>: the times you can't work, or would rather work, each week.</li>
        </ul>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Add your shifts to your calendar">
        <ol>
            <li>Select <strong>Add to calendar</strong> at the top of My Shifts.</li>
            <li>Follow the steps for Google, Apple or Outlook, or copy the link into your calendar app's
                "Subscribe" or "Add calendar from URL" option.</li>
        </ol>
        <FluentText>
            Your calendar then keeps itself up to date as the rota changes.
        </FluentText>
        <HelpNote Intent="MessageBarIntent.Warning">
            The link is only shown once, and anyone who has it can see your shifts. If you lose it or think
            someone else has it, get a new one from the same place. The old link then stops working.
        </HelpNote>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
